WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.916
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.916 --> 00:00:03.011
Tuesday the third, Election Day.

00:00:03.011 --> 00:00:09.164
Calendar runs long today, from a run first thing to a late class that ends around nine-thirty.

00:00:09.164 --> 00:00:11.586
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:11.586 --> 00:00:16.757
When a loud sound hides a quieter one close to it in pitch, what's that called?

00:00:16.757 --> 00:00:18.066
Answer's at the end.

00:00:18.066 --> 00:00:19.506
Quick note on weather.

00:00:19.506 --> 00:00:25.070
The forecast sources wouldn't load this morning, so this one's on you and the window.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:25.987
Okay, the day.

00:00:25.987 --> 00:00:29.849
Calendar has an easy zone two run at seven, thirty minutes.

00:00:29.849 --> 00:00:31.681
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:31.681 --> 00:00:35.216
There's a UPS returns drop on there at quarter to six.

00:00:35.216 --> 00:00:41.042
And the calendar closes with yin and nidra at eight twenty-five, wrapping at nine-thirty.

00:00:41.042 --> 00:00:44.773
So there's a real gap between the drop-off and the class.

00:00:44.773 --> 00:00:50.075
That's the window to eat, and polls stay open till nine if you haven't voted yet.

00:00:50.075 --> 00:00:50.991
Alright, news.

00:00:50.991 --> 00:00:55.442
Short one today, because the news sources were down on this end too.

00:00:55.442 --> 00:00:59.108
The only thing worth saying is that tonight's the night.

00:00:59.108 --> 00:01:05.654
Polls close at nine, and the governor's race and those ballot proposals start coming in right after.

00:01:05.654 --> 00:01:10.367
By the time you're rolling up your mat, the first numbers should be out.

00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:12.920
The full rundown can wait for tomorrow.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.145
Today's challenge is a social one.

00:01:15.145 --> 00:01:22.542
At some point during the workday, ask one person a real question about something they're good at outside of work.

00:01:22.542 --> 00:01:25.291
A hobby, a skill, a thing they used to do.

00:01:25.291 --> 00:01:28.040
Then let them talk, and ask one follow-up.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:28.695
That's it.

00:01:28.695 --> 00:01:36.288
Most people rarely get asked, and you'll probably learn something you didn't expect about someone you see every day.

00:01:36.288 --> 00:01:38.710
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:45.648
Remember setting up the memo's private feed path, the idea that a link nobody can guess works like a lock?

00:01:45.648 --> 00:01:50.099
Last week the answer was that leaks are the real risk, not guessing.

00:01:50.099 --> 00:01:55.532
Here's why guessing is basically off the table, and where leaks actually come from.

00:01:55.532 --> 00:01:57.103
Start with the guessing.

00:01:57.103 --> 00:02:02.274
A good random link carries about a hundred and twenty-eight bits of randomness.

00:02:02.274 --> 00:02:05.285
That's around twenty-two characters of jumble.

00:02:05.285 --> 00:02:10.064
The number of possible links is a three with thirty-eight zeros after it.

00:02:10.064 --> 00:02:13.402
Say someone could try a billion links every second.

00:02:13.402 --> 00:02:20.275
Running through them all would take something like a trillion times longer than the universe has existed.

00:02:20.275 --> 00:02:22.763
Even being lucky only gets you so far.

00:02:22.763 --> 00:02:25.774
So a long random link really can't be guessed.

00:02:25.774 --> 00:02:31.338
A short one, or one built from a pattern like a date or a name, is a different story.

00:02:31.338 --> 00:02:34.218
Now the leaks, and a few of them are sneaky.

00:02:34.218 --> 00:02:35.985
The first is link previews.

00:02:35.985 --> 00:02:42.924
Paste a link into most chat apps and the app's servers go fetch it, just to draw that little preview card.

00:02:42.924 --> 00:02:47.178
Nobody clicked, but a computer you don't control has now seen it.

00:02:47.178 --> 00:02:49.731
The second is the trail a click leaves.

00:02:49.731 --> 00:02:55.426
When you tap a link on a page, your browser can tell the next site where you came from.

00:02:55.426 --> 00:03:03.870
Modern browsers mostly trim that down to just the site's name, but older setups and some apps still pass along the whole address.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.946
The third is being linked from anywhere public.

00:03:06.946 --> 00:03:12.183
If the link ever lands on a public page, search engines can find it and list it.

00:03:12.183 --> 00:03:19.645
There's a polite request you can add to a page asking search engines to stay away, but it's a request, not a wall.

00:03:19.645 --> 00:03:21.740
And the last one is the big one.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.507
You can't take a link back.

00:03:23.507 --> 00:03:26.060
Once it's out, there's no unsending it.

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:28.285
The only fix is changing the lock.

00:03:28.285 --> 00:03:33.980
That means making a fresh random link, moving the thing there, and killing the old one.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.795
Anyone you still want in gets the new link.

00:03:36.795 --> 00:03:38.759
So the honest version is this.

00:03:38.759 --> 00:03:42.228
An unguessable link is a strong lock with no key log.

00:03:42.228 --> 00:03:46.221
It's great for things where a leak would be a little awkward.

00:03:46.221 --> 00:03:56.301
For things where a leak would really hurt, you want a real login, so you can see who's coming in and shut one person out without changing it for everyone.

00:03:56.301 --> 00:04:03.763
So: calendar says run at seven, Godinger till five, the UPS drop at five forty-five, and yin at eight twenty-five.

00:04:03.763 --> 00:04:06.185
And ask one person about their thing.

00:04:06.185 --> 00:04:07.494
The answer: masking.

00:04:07.494 --> 00:04:09.000
Have an easy run, Ziad.
